{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.HolonomySpectrumSignObstruction"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.hecke_eigenvalues_not_unit_modulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.hecke_eigenvalues_not_unit_modulus","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator.lean#L116-L130","doc":"**Route B dissolves the scale-fixing.** An elliptic generator forces\nunit-modulus braiding eigenvalues, but Kohno–Drinfeld fixes `ħ = 2 ln q` by\nmatching the Hecke pair `{q, −q⁻¹}`, and for `q > 1` neither member has modulus\none. So a non-Hermitian deformation buys ellipticity at the cost of the matching\nthat made the construction Kohno–Drinfeld in the first place. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.no_complex_q_gives_real_subunit_character","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.no_complex_q_gives_real_subunit_character","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator.lean#L98-L114","doc":"**No complex `q` gives a real character below 1.** If the character is real —\nwhich `Q_β` being real forces — then `q` is real, and the character is then\n`(x + x⁻¹)/2 ≥ 1`. So allowing `Im q ≠ 0` and keeping the real part does not\nrescue the holonomy reading; it only trades a wrong-sided real number for a\ncomplex one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.character_im_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.character_im_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator.lean#L79-L96","doc":"**The character's imaginary part vanishes only for real `q`.** Writing\n`q = x + iy`, `Im[(q + q⁻¹)/2] = (y/2)(1 − 1/s)` with `s = x² + y²`; for `x > 1`\nwe have `s > 1`, so the factor `1 − 1/s` is strictly positive and the product is\nzero exactly when `y = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.abs_sq_gt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.abs_sq_gt_one","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ImQCannotSupplyEllipticGenerator.lean#L73-L77","doc":"With `x > 1`, the modulus-squared `s = x² + y²` exceeds 1 whatever `y` is.\nHence `ln q` is never purely imaginary on the substrate branch, and a purely\nelliptic Kohno–Drinfeld generator is impossible outright. ","signatureChunk":0}]}